# @trace spec:ci-release, plan 1324-ujvb
#
# THE TRUST ANCHOR for cosign itself. One record, no logic.
#
# WHY A PIN AND NOT "download the latest release". A provenance tool installed
# without provenance is this row's defect one level up: a cosign fetched from
# whatever the release API answers today can verify every artifact we ship and
# be, itself, unverified. The pin is what makes the FIRST link checkable — the
# only link that cannot be checked by the tool it is bootstrapping.
#
# ONE HASH, EVERY PLATFORM. The anchor is `cosign_checksums.txt`, the release's
# own manifest, and the per-binary hashes are read OUT of it once it has been
# checked against `checksums_sha256` below. The first version of this file
# pinned a single binary's hash and hardcoded `asset=cosign-linux-amd64` —
# lenovinha's review found that on a Mac the download succeeded, the hash
# matched, chmod succeeded, and the self-verification then EXECUTED A LINUX
# BINARY, reporting `cosign:verification-failed:sigstore`. A host that cannot
# run the file would have reported a SIGNATURE FAILURE: the could-not-run /
# failed conflation this script polices everywhere else, committed inside it.
#
# Pinning four hashes would have fixed the platform bug and created a rot
# surface — four values to re-derive per release, any one of which can be
# quietly stale. One manifest hash covers every platform and every future one,
# and the manifest is itself signed.
#
# WHY THE SHA256 IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN, and this is the part easy to
# implement past. A hash proves the bytes match what whoever wrote this line
# saw. It says nothing about whether those bytes are what sigstore published —
# if the pin was copied from a compromised page, the hash check passes and looks
# like diligence. So the hash gates EXECUTION, and the executed cosign then
# verifies the manifest's OWN signature against sigstore's certificate and
# transparency log. Two different questions:
#
#   sha256   "are these the bytes the pin names?"     -> may I run this at all
#   sigstore "did sigstore publish these bytes?"      -> is the pin itself honest
#
# Neither subsumes the other. Running only the hash trusts this file; running
# only the signature means executing an unverified binary to ask it whether it
# should have been executed.
#
# THE IDENTITY IS READ FROM THE CERTIFICATE, NOT ASSUMED. cosign's releases are
# NOT signed by a GitHub Actions workflow identity, which is the plausible guess
# and is wrong — they are signed by a GCP service account through Google's OIDC
# issuer. Pinning the guess would fail verification for the wrong reason and
# read as a broken download. Verified 2026-09-22 by decoding the bundle's
# certificate:
#     X509v3 Subject Alternative Name: critical
#         email:keyless@projectsigstore.iam.gserviceaccount.com
#     1.3.6.1.4.1.57264.1.1: https://accounts.google.com
#
# TO ROLL THIS FORWARD: change the version, download the new
# cosign_checksums.txt and its .sigstore.json, re-read the certificate identity
# (do not carry those two lines over on faith — an upstream signing-identity
# change is exactly the event this file exists to notice), recompute
# checksums_sha256, and re-run scripts/test-ensure-cosign.sh, whose negative
# controls must still reject and whose platform arm must still refuse an
# unsupported host rather than failing verification on it.
#
# A PLATFORM NOT LISTED IN `platforms` IS A could-not-run, NEVER A FAILURE.
# That is the whole point of the list: an unknown host must say it cannot do
# this, not that cosign's signature is bad.
#
# Fields are `key=value`, one per line, `#` comments ignored.

version=v3.1.3
checksums=cosign_checksums.txt
checksums_sha256=aec2a6f68d307b09ae196e388dc691a146fa8bdba7fcce9ca4ca41b918adfa63
certificate_identity=keyless@projectsigstore.iam.gserviceaccount.com
certificate_oidc_issuer=https://accounts.google.com

# `<uname -s>/<uname -m> = <release asset>`, lowercased. Hosts outside this list
# report could-not-run. x86_64 and amd64, aarch64 and arm64, are the same
# machines under different names and both spellings are listed rather than
# normalised in code — the mapping is data, and a reader can check it.
platform=linux/x86_64=cosign-linux-amd64
platform=linux/amd64=cosign-linux-amd64
platform=linux/aarch64=cosign-linux-arm64
platform=linux/arm64=cosign-linux-arm64
platform=darwin/x86_64=cosign-darwin-amd64
platform=darwin/amd64=cosign-darwin-amd64
platform=darwin/arm64=cosign-darwin-arm64
